/**
 * The SECOND fresh holdout, frozen before the detector saw it.
 *
 * Holdout 1 stopped being a holdout the moment one of its findings was fixed — the same argument
 * that demoted the 120-case development corpus, applied to itself rather than argued around. Its
 * first run (85/105 FN, 32/100 FP) stands as the only unbiased number it will ever produce; its
 * second run after the bounded repair (70/105, 32/100) is a regression measurement.
 *
 * So generalisation moves here. 224 cases, 112 MUST_REFUSE across the seven prohibitions and 112
 * MUST_ALLOW across the seven research categories, 56 English and 56 Korean on each side.
 *
 * ## Independence, again, and checked
 *
 * Generated by `gpt-5.6-sol` through `codex exec -s read-only` on included capacity, from
 * `docs/LEGAL_GUARDRAILS.md` and a product description alone, forbidden from opening `src/`,
 * `tests/` or `scripts/`; transcripts checked afterwards, no access. **Zero exact overlap and zero
 * punctuation-insensitive overlap with holdout 1** — measured, not assumed, because a second
 * holdout that repeats the first measures nothing new.
 *
 * The generator was NOT told what holdout 1 found. Telling it "loss protection and price
 * prediction were missed" would have produced a targeted retest of the known weaknesses, which is
 * a different and much weaker claim than generalisation.
 *
 * This run asked for registers the first did not: fragments, context-then-ask, typos, mobile
 * brevity, over-politeness, Korean 반말 alongside 존댓말. Several allow cases pre-empt the
 * guardrail in words — "I'm checking the guidance, not asking", "앞으로 오를지 말지는 필요 없어",
 * "No prediction here" — which is how careful users actually write to a system they expect to
 * refuse them.
 *
 * Every label was reviewed against the seven prohibitions before this file was committed, and the
 * commit that adds it contains no result.
 */

